<p>A friend of mine claims that "the wheel exists" and we shouldn't spend time reinventing it.</p>
<p>This is true.  Up to a point.</p>
<p>If you are in the transport business, or even the business of making wagons, you should not be reinventing the wheel.  You should be in the business of using them.</p>
<p>But what if you are in the wheel business?  Taking to its logical extreme, the advice that "the wheel exists" means that once <i>any</i> wheel exists, there is no room for improvement - and neither the spoked wheel or the rubber tyre would have been invented.</p>
<p>As far as technology goes, and certainly when we are talking about software, I think I am always in the "innovation" business - the "this could be done better" business - the "why is this so broken business".  This is not to diminish the work of those who build things that we can all use on a daily basis, but with a view that software development <i>in particular</i>, and most software <i>in general</i> could be better "if only".</p>
<p>When I was younger, I thought that was what people meant by "fundamental research".  But I spent a few years of my life trying to do that, and discovered that what they meant was even more "fundamental" than I had the stomach for.  Obtaining a deep, deep understanding of the mathematical underpinnings of everything we do.  In real life, producing reliable software quickly and repeatedly is important; in the research community, <i>providing a convincing proof</i> of the "correctness" of software is the only thing that matters.  You may have to actually do it to understand the ocean (certainly more than a gulf) between these two.  So I don't have a home there either.</p>
<p>So this post is just a self-justificatory explanation of why I do many of the things that I do.</p>
<h2><p>I Think a Lot
</h2>
<p>This probably sounds very hubristic, but I believe I think a lot.  Possibly more than other people.</p>
<p>Experience has taught me that I definitely do not think more deeply than <i>anyone</i> else; I have met any number of people who can think more deeply than me about issues.  And I am always impressed when I read books by authors such as Orwell at how deeply they have thought about things in a way that I could not have.</p>
<p>But very rarely do I accept "surface level" explanations, but I tend to ask "why?" and then not stop.  I have commented elsewhere that this is akin to peeling back layer after layer of an onion: inside, you see a different onion, but it is very similar to the first, but contains less assumptions.</p>
<p>In part, this just helps me understand why the world is the way it is.  As an example, when I was studying formal logic at University, the setup for predicate logic seemed insanely complicated.  Starting from the "end", where there were just relations and predicates, I worked backwards, trying to understand how I would do it differently and more simply ... until I ended up having come back to a system that was isomorphic to what I had been taught, just using different language.  Ah.</p>
<p>Much of the time, "incremental improvement" brings benefits to any system we are working on.  But there are cases where incremental improvement cannot get you where you want to go.  An analogy I have seen presented is of trying to get as high as possible.  The "incremental improvement" approach is the same as saying "always walk upwards".  This will take you to the highest point of land where you are, but it won't allow you to reach the top of any <i>other</i> hill or mountain - which might be higher.  In order to do that, you need to walk <i>down</i>, possibly all the way to the bottom, and start up a different hill.</p>
<p>Maybe "walking up different hills" is a better title for this article than "reinventing the wheel".</p>
<h2><p>Memory Allocation
</h2>
<p>As an example of this in practice - all of which has nothing to do with me - let us consider the question of memory allocation.</p>
<p>In the earliest computer programs, memory was allocated <i>explicitly</i> using addresses.  That is, if you had an integer variable, you would say "it is the one at address 0x205".  You would then copy that into register 4, add the contents of register 3, and then save it back to 0x205.  I kid you not.</p>
<p>A major step forward was taken when somebody said "we could give that a name".  Now, the address 0x205 can be called "SPEED_VAR".  The actual execution of the program has not changed, but it is now clearer (to humans) what is going on.</p>
<p>As function calls came along, and a stack was used to track those, it became possible to also store variables "on the stack".  Basically, this is an indexing technique where there is a register <tt>SP</tt> which cane be used to point into arbitrary memory and then a value can be pulled from (say) <tt>SP+6</tt>.  This enables functions to have local variables (whether parameters or just declared locally) which can have "floating" addresses.  And it also allowed functions to be re-entrant and recursive (check it out, kids: this was not true of early FORTRAN compilers in the 1950s).</p>
<p>And then along came heap allocation, typified by the <tt>malloc</tt> function of C, in which it was possible to obtain a chunk of memory with no fixed address and not on the stack.  Whoa!  For all the problems we have had with <tt>malloc</tt> and <tt>free</tt>, in its time this was a radical notion.</p>
<p>But, given that we had those problems, we were able to fix them by introducing the idea of a <i>garbage collector</i>, which took over the job of freeing up unused memory.  By doing this, it enabled us to just say "I want a new one of these" and then when we forgot about it, it went away.</p>
<p>Each of these was a reinvented wheel: it was fundamentally and dramatically different from what had come before.  At each point, minor improvements were made along the way (for example, the difference between <tt>malloc</tt> and <tt>free</tt> in C and <tt>new</tt> and <tt>delete</tt> in C++), but without a change in concept or metaphor, further, dramatic progress could not have been made.</p>
<p>It is that change of metaphor which I think is important to reinventing the wheel.</p>
<h2><p>Choosing a Viewpoint
</h2>
<p>With any solution to any problem, there are two salient characteristics: the problem domain and your viewpoint.</p>
<p>The problem domain is, in some sense, fairly fixed: "I want to pull a variable from memory". What changes is your viewpoint.</p>
<p>Although I am increasingly convinced that viewpoints are somewhat inherent to us as individuals. Mine, for example, always prioritizes consistency, simplicity and whenever possible getting the machine to do the work. I like to describe this as "laziness": I want to get the most results out of the least amount of effort.</p>
<p>I also have a general dissatisfaction built into my viewpoint: almost anything that already exists is not good enough can be improved. If an incremental improvement is possible, I will try that. But often, that just isn't the case.</p>
<p>So, what do I do?</p>
<p>The first thing I do is try and take several steps backwards.  Rather than asking "what don't I like about this solution?", I try to ask, "what is the problem I am really trying to solve?".  Or to paraphrase Kennedy, "I dream of things that have never been, and ask 'why not?'"</p>
<p>The second thing I do is worry at the problem.  I don't try to sit down and "write some code".  Rather, I will give time to thinking about the problem in the abstract.  Walking alone - either during a morning commute, or out in the country - is always a good time for this (watching cricket also provides an excellent environment for calm, undistracted thinking).  There are relatively few distractions and the temptation to "just try something" is not there.  Some problems are solved easily and I can walk to work and arrive ready to put a solution into practice (once, when struggling with the <tt>maven</tt> build tool, I figured out how to do the same thing more efficiently and reliably on the way into the office; by the time I was walking home, I had a basic implementation functioning).  On other occasions, months or years go by before lightning strikes (the seed behind ChainLedger was planted in 2013 or so, but only developed fully in 2024).  Other problems, such as a fully automated software assistant, have been preying on my mind for decades without solution.</p>
<p>When thinking about simplicity, P.J. Landin's   <a href='https://www.cs.cmu.edu/~crary/819-f09/Landin66.pdf'>"ISWIM"</a> language is always a great inspiration to me.  While not in the paper I've just cited, my recollection is of him saying something like "if you used the correct programming language, every program would just be 'go'" - if you see what I mean (hence ISWIM). The logical consequence of this, of course, is that the programming language should do more to meet the programmer where they are, and take on more and more of the "grunt work".  This has so many advantages for the user that it is hard to enumerate them all, but most obviously, it is quicker and easier to get things done; it is harder to make mistakes; it is clearer to others after the event what <i>was</i> done.</p>
<p>Whenever I am considering both a problem and its solution, I look for consistency amongst the parts.  If I can manage to see two things as "the same", that halves the amount of code I need to write.  If I can make two things behave in a similar way, it reduces the amount of work a user has to do.</p>
<p>I always like to look at, and borrow from, other domains.  If I can see a parallel between the problem I have, and a problem someone else has solved in a different domain, I always try and look at that for inspiration.  Historians, for example, put things into timelines: can I do the same with this information?  Focus is always important, so how much redundant information can I remove: an email inbox has thousands of messages on display; a physical inbox just has a "top" item; can I duplicate that, so a user is not overwhelmed but focused on that one item?</p>
<p>Given all that, I hypothesize a possible solution: how would this work?  I like to take an example, and work my way through it.  If everything seems OK in my head, I try and write down - or draw out - what the solution implies for a problem.  Does this seem reasonable?  If so, maybe I should try it.  If not, let me think about this some more.  If I'm not making any progress, let me put it on the back burner until inspiration strikes - or more likely, I think about it again in a <i>different</i> context: one that has the vital piece I am missing right now.</p>
<h2><p>Experiment
</h2>
<p>The cost of doing something is directly proportional to how much effort you put in.</p>
<p>I like to distinguish between "production" and "experiments".  I like to think that if I can experiment with something for a day or a week, I can see if this is something worth pursuing.  Since 2019 I have tended to do this "in public" on my  <a href='https://ignorancemaybestrength.blogspot.com/'>blog</a>.  With that experiment complete - and documented - I can go back at any time and review both the ideas that I had and a summary of what worked and what did not.  I can then repeat the experiment - with changes, of course - and see what I come up with.</p>
<p>And it is no sign of weakness to give up.  As with admitting ignorance, admitting defeat can be a strength.  Theoretically, you must have learned something, because you now know it's not worth continuing, and there are problems with your approach.  This knowledge you have gained can go back into your thought process and you can start the whole process over again - choosing a new viewpoint and approach.  Next time you may be successful - or you may not.</p>
<h2><p>Conclusion
</h2>
<p>"Reinventing the Wheel" makes no sense if you are just using wheels.  But it has its place in making the world better if you are focused on the quality of wheels on offer.  If you think you can build a wheel that is better than the wheels on offer - and makes for more efficient transportation - go for it!</p>
